# CLAUDE.md

## Project: [PROJECT_NAME]

Stack: Directus + Next.js (App Router)

## Architecture

- **Data Layer**: Directus at [DIRECTUS_URL]
- **Interface + Logic Layer**: Next.js App Router
- **Local Dev**: Docker Compose (Directus + PostgreSQL + Redis), see the `directus-dev` plugin
- **Deployment**: see project deployment plugin (e.g. `dokploy-dev`, `vercel-dev`)

## Key Files

- `lib/directus.ts` — Directus SDK client, server-only
- `lib/content.ts` — every Directus read of the pages, tagged with its collections
- `types/directus.ts` — TypeScript schema matching the Directus collections
- `app/api/revalidate/route.ts` — expires a cache tag when a Directus Flow calls it
- `app/` — Next.js App Router pages

## Conventions

- The SDK has no `cache` option. Directus reads go through `lib/content.ts`, tagged with the collections they read (previous caching model: `withOptions` with `next.tags`; Cache Components: `'use cache'` with `cacheTag`)
- `DIRECTUS_ADMIN_TOKEN` is server-side only (`import 'server-only'`) and belongs to a dedicated user, not an administrator
- File URLs never carry a token: public files, or the asset proxy route
- Never cache a read made with a signed-in user's token
- A Server Action is a public endpoint that takes any id: authenticate with `requireUser()` and authorize. Write with the user's own token (`withToken(session.accessToken, ...)`) so Directus applies that user's policies, or check role or ownership in code when only the server token is used
- After a schema change: update `types/directus.ts`, then run `npx tsc --noEmit`
- After a mutation: `updateTag(collection)`; content edited in Directus is expired by the Flow through `/api/revalidate`
- Liveness check for Directus is `/server/ping` (the health endpoint needs a token)

